#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3499a528-3b5b-5342-a45f-58ae5606f8f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354862} {:answer/id #uuid "13e70303-6e01-5cc5-bb94-b26acfeeebfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|+y=2{x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354863} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b648b8-3bba-5404-bcfd-aefa9938d0b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|+y={x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354864} {:answer/id #uuid "6024ef2e-28f1-5f1a-b820-5f13c5c76835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y={x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354865} {:answer/id #uuid "83146c73-c536-5451-b0e3-e6238f76276e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y=2{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354866} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b3dbd0-63f9-594d-94cb-6b783e0bc8a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No differentiable classical solution exists." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0b3dbd0-63f9-594d-94cb-6b783e0bc8a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No differentiable classical solution exists." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354875} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc7ecdf4-9edf-52dc-b394-d454fba788f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1428f3d-5054-5866-8041-e9c9eed9b449" :question/math-academy-id "q-104058" :question/problem "Determine whether a differentiable classical solution on an open interval containing the initial point exists for the differential equation $(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "A classical solution must be differentiable on an interval containing $x=1$ and satisfy the differential equation at that point. Substituting $x=1$ and $y(1)=1$ gives\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{1}{1\\cdot1}-\\frac11\\right)y'(1)=2,\\qquad 0\\cdot y'(1)=2.$$\n\nThis is impossible for a finite derivative. Therefore no differentiable classical solution exists for the stated initial condition.\n\nFormal separation on nonsingular regions gives\n\n$$(1/y-1)\\,dy=2x\\,dx,\\qquad \\ln|y|-y=x^2+C.$$\n\nRequiring the integrated level set to contain $(1,1)$ yields $C=-2$, hence $\\ln|y|-y=x^2-2$. This is the source's formally integrated relation, but it is not a classical solution of the IVP. Differentiating it at $(1,1)$ again requires $0\\cdot y'(1)=2$. Near $y=1$, continuity would give $y>0$, and $\\ln y-y\\le -1$ makes $x^2\\le1$ necessary on that level set, so it cannot supply a graph on a two-sided neighborhood of $x=1$ either.\n\nThe source capture and its accepted formal relation are retained for provenance. The corrected study answer concerns the original IVP's existence."}]}
